---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import '../../styles/packmetry-workspace.css';
import '../../styles/examples.css';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Mixed Item Packing Example — Packmetry"
  description="A worked Packmetry benchmark example: one 80 mm cube and five 20 mm cubes packed into a 100 mm cube carton, with canonical utilization math and verification boundaries."
>
  <main class="pm-example-page">
    <div class="pm-example-shell">
      <header class="pm-example-hero">
        <p class="pm-example-kicker">
          Worked benchmark example
        </p>

        <h1>
          One large item,
          five small items,
          one carton.
        </h1>

        <p>
          This example uses Packmetry&apos;s locked
          <strong>highly-different-item-sizes</strong>
          benchmark case. It shows what the benchmark
          expects, how canonical volume metrics are
          derived, and what the result does — and
          does not — prove.
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="pm-example-source">
        <strong>Repository source</strong>

        <p>
          The benchmark case lives in
          <code>src/test/benchmarks/cases.ts</code>.
          Its expected outcome is feasible packing
          with one carton, six placed item instances
          and zero unplaced instances.
        </p>
      </div>

      <section class="pm-example-inputs">
        <div class="pm-example-section-heading">
          <p class="pm-example-kicker">
            Inputs
          </p>

          <h2>
            A deliberately uneven mix of item sizes.
          </h2>

          <p>
            The benchmark uses canonical millimetres,
            unrestricted rotation and the benchmark
            helper&apos;s fewest-cartons objective.
            It does not provide item weights, carton
            tare weight, external carton dimensions
            or DIM settings.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="pm-example-table-wrap">
          <table class="pm-example-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Input</th>
                <th>Dimensions</th>
                <th>Quantity</th>
                <th>Role</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>

            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>
                  <strong>Large item</strong>
                </td>
                <td>80 × 80 × 80 mm</td>
                <td>1</td>
                <td>Requested item</td>
              </tr>

              <tr>
                <td>
                  <strong>Small item</strong>
                </td>
                <td>20 × 20 × 20 mm</td>
                <td>5</td>
                <td>Requested item</td>
              </tr>

              <tr>
                <td>
                  <strong>Carton</strong>
                </td>
                <td>100 × 100 × 100 mm</td>
                <td>As needed by the plan</td>
                <td>Internal packing space</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="pm-example-result">
        <div class="pm-example-section-heading">
          <p class="pm-example-kicker">
            Locked benchmark expectation
          </p>

          <h2>
            All six item instances fit in one carton.
          </h2>

          <p>
            The benchmark expectation records a
            feasible result with one used carton,
            six placed instances and no unplaced
            items.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="pm-example-result-grid">
          <article>
            <span>Status</span>
            <strong>Feasible</strong>
          </article>

          <article>
            <span>Cartons used</span>
            <strong>1</strong>
          </article>

          <article>
            <span>Items placed</span>
            <strong>6</strong>
          </article>

          <article>
            <span>Items unplaced</span>
            <strong>0</strong>
          </article>
        </div>

        <div class="pm-example-math">
          <article>
            <h3>Requested item volume</h3>

            <p>
              Large item:
              <code>
                80 × 80 × 80 = 512,000 mm³
              </code>
            </p>

            <p>
              Five small items:
              <code>
                5 × 20 × 20 × 20 = 40,000 mm³
              </code>
            </p>

            <p>
              Total:
              <code>
                552,000 mm³
              </code>
            </p>
          </article>

          <article>
            <h3>Canonical carton metrics</h3>

            <p>
              Carton volume:
              <code>
                100 × 100 × 100 = 1,000,000 mm³
              </code>
            </p>

            <p>
              Empty volume:
              <code>
                1,000,000 - 552,000 = 448,000 mm³
              </code>
            </p>

            <p>
              Volume utilization:
              <code>
                552,000 / 1,000,000 = 55.2%
              </code>
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="pm-example-explanation">
        <div class="pm-example-section-heading">
          <p class="pm-example-kicker">
            What the example teaches
          </p>

          <h2>
            Volume is useful, but geometry still
            decides whether the plan is valid.
          </h2>
        </div>

        <div class="pm-example-prose">
          <section>
            <h3>
              55.2% utilization does not prove fit.
            </h3>

            <p>
              The volume arithmetic tells us how
              much internal carton volume is occupied
              after a plan exists. It cannot prove
              that a collection of three-dimensional
              items can be placed without overlap
              or boundary violations.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section>
            <h3>
              The benchmark locks the outcome,
              not a universal placement recipe.
            </h3>

            <p>
              The case records the expected status,
              carton count and placed/unplaced item
              counts. Production planning still
              relies on solver-generated placements
              and the independent verifier rather
              than treating volume arithmetic as a
              substitute for placement geometry.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section>
            <h3>
              No weight or DIM conclusion belongs
              to this case.
            </h3>

            <p>
              The benchmark does not supply item
              weights, carton tare weight, external
              carton dimensions or dimensional-weight
              settings. Packmetry therefore should
              not invent gross-weight, DIM-weight or
              shipping-price conclusions for it.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section>
            <h3>
              One carton is the benchmark result,
              not a global-optimality proof.
            </h3>

            <p>
              The current baseline is deterministic
              and bounded. This example shows the
              locked expected outcome for this test
              case; it does not turn the baseline
              heuristic into an exhaustive proof of
              mathematical optimality.
            </p>
          </section>
        </div>

        <div class="pm-example-boundary">
          <strong>Trust boundary</strong>

          <p>
            To understand how candidate generation,
            independent verification, objective
            ranking and canonical-plan construction
            fit together, read the
            <a href="/methodology/packing-algorithm/">
              packing algorithm methodology
            </a>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="pm-example-cta">
        <div>
          <p class="pm-example-kicker">
            Try your own case
          </p>

          <h2>
            Replace the benchmark dimensions with
            the items and cartons you actually have.
          </h2>

          <p>
            Personal is the simpler route for
            everyday packing. Business adds carton
            inventory, costs, weights and DIM
            settings while using the same core
            planning architecture.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="pm-example-cta-actions">
          <a href="/personal/">
            Personal workspace
          </a>

          <a
            class="pm-example-secondary-link"
            href="/business/"
          >
            Business workspace
          </a>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
